# PROJECT: BUILDSENSE
## AI-Enabled Building Condition Monitoring & Predictive Maintenance System

Act as a senior full-stack architect, AI/ML engineer, civil-infrastructure domain expert, UI/UX designer, database engineer, and QA engineer.

Build a complete, interactive, professional web application called **BuildSense**, an AI-enabled building condition monitoring, construction quality assurance, defect detection, and predictive maintenance platform.

The application will initially be demonstrated to stakeholders as a working prototype, but its architecture must support subsequent production development.

Do not generate only a landing page, static dashboard, collection of disconnected screens, or wireframe. Build a functional application with working navigation, persistent data, real image analysis using Gemini where supported, a consistent database, role-based interfaces, operational workflows, charts, reports, and meaningful interactions.

Do not stop after generating the frontend. Implement the backend, data models, API endpoints, persistence, validation, error handling, and integration points as well.

Use the requirements below as the authoritative specification.

# 1. PRODUCT VISION

BuildSense transforms building maintenance from reactive repairs into proactive, risk-based, predictive asset management.

The platform must support the complete lifecycle:

Construction quality inspection → Building registration → Field inspection → AI defect detection → Engineer verification → Building Condition Index calculation → Risk assessment → Maintenance prioritisation → Work-order assignment → Repair evidence → Engineer verification → Reinspection → Updated condition history → Predictive maintenance planning.

Additional workflows:
- Occupants report defects using building QR codes.
- Construction teams record quality checks and failed material tests.
- Senior management monitors buildings, divisions, risks, budgets, and maintenance performance.
- Auditors inspect the complete history of observations, decisions, and repairs.

All observations must be linked to a common building and element record.

The platform must never automatically make final structural safety decisions. Qualified engineers must verify significant findings.

